# Tutorial 01 — Dialogue Basics
# Learn how dialogue lines work, how to use text tags, and how the narrator voice differs from characters.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_01_dialogue
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Welcome to your first lesson!
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: This one's all about the most fundamental thing in a visual novel — dialogue.
Lavender: By the end, you'll know how to write character lines, narrator lines, and dress them up with text tags.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Pick a topic, or jump straight to the practice scene to try things yourself.
> How dialogue lines work -> section_lines
> The narrator voice -> section_narrator
> Text tags — bold, italic, color -> section_tags
> Combining tags creatively -> section_combos
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Character Dialogue ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_lines
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The basic pattern is dead simple. You write a character name, a colon, then what they say.
narrator: Like this — every line you've been reading from me follows that exact format.
Lavender: In the script file, it looks like this:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Hello, world!{/color}
[show lavender left happy]
Lavender: That's it. The engine handles the text box, the name label, everything.
Lavender: One line of script becomes one click of dialogue for the player.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can have as many characters as you want — just declare them with {b}@character{/b} in your definitions file.
Lavender: For this project, characters live in {b}scripts/definitions/characters.vns{/b}.
> Next: The narrator -> section_narrator
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Narrator ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_narrator
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: There's a special character name the engine treats differently — {b}narrator{/b}.
narrator: When I speak, there's no name displayed in the text box. Just the words.
narrator: Narration is great for scene descriptions, internal thoughts, or stage directions the player should read.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: In the script, it looks just like character dialogue:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The room fell silent.{/color}
Lavender: The engine knows to hide the name label for narrator lines. Clean and simple.
narrator: You're reading narrator lines right now, by the way.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Mix character lines and narrator lines freely — that push and pull is what gives scenes their rhythm.
> Next: Text tags -> section_tags
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Text Tags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_tags
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Now let's make text more expressive. VNS supports {b}inline tags{/b} you can wrap around words.
Lavender: The basics — {b}bold{/b}, {i}italic{/i}, and {b}{i}both together{/i}{/b}.
narrator: In the script: {color=#8888cc}\{b\}bold\{/b\} and \{i\}italic\{/i\}{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can also color individual words:
Lavender: Like this — {color=#ff6666}danger{/color}, {color=#66ff66}safety{/color}, {color=#6688ff}mystery{/color}.
narrator: In the script: {color=#8888cc}\{color=#ff6666\}danger\{/color\}{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And for flair, there's {wave}wavy text{/wave} and {shake}shaky text{/shake}.
Lavender: Use them sparingly — a little goes a long way. You don't want every line doing backflips.
> Next: Combining tags -> section_combos
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Combining Tags ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_combos
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Tags nest inside each other just fine.
Lavender: Want bold red text? {b}{color=#ff4444}Like this.{/color}{/b}
Lavender: How about italic wavy text? {i}{wave}Spooky.{/wave}{/i}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: A few guidelines from experience:
Lavender: Don't stack more than two or three tags deep — the script gets hard to read fast.
Lavender: Color tags work best for {color=#4a9eff}key words{/color} or {color=#ff8844}emotional beats{/color}, not entire paragraphs.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And remember, the player will read this at whatever text speed is set.
Lavender: Fancy formatting means nothing if the pacing is off — but that's a topic for Tutorial 02.
> Quick reference -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's your cheat sheet. Keep this file open while you write!
narrator: {b}Character line:{/b}  CharacterName: What they say.
narrator: {b}Narrator line:{/b}  narrator: Description or inner thought.
narrator: {b}Bold:{/b}  \{b\}text\{/b\}  →  {b}text{/b}
narrator: {b}Italic:{/b}  \{i\}text\{/i\}  →  {i}text{/i}
narrator: {b}Color:{/b}  \{color=#hex\}text\{/color\}  →  {color=#4a9eff}text{/color}
narrator: {b}Wave:{/b}  \{wave\}text\{/wave\}  →  {wave}text{/wave}
narrator: {b}Shake:{/b}  \{shake\}text\{/shake\}  →  {shake}text{/shake}
narrator: {b}Nesting:{/b}  \{b\}\{color=#ff0000\}text\{/color\}\{/b\}  →  {b}{color=#ff0000}text{/color}{/b}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That covers the essentials. Everything else is just building on these patterns.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Alright, let's put it together! I'll set up a tiny scene — pick the tone you want to try.
> Write something cheerful -> practice_cheerful
> Write something dramatic -> practice_dramatic
> Write something mysterious -> practice_mystery

@label practice_cheerful
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: {color=#66dd88}Perfect day for an adventure!{/color}
narrator: Sunlight poured through the windows, warm and golden.
Lavender: See? A bright color for her mood, and a narrator line to paint the setting.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: In the script, that was just three lines — one show command, one character line, one narrator line.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_dramatic
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: {b}You can't be serious.{/b}
narrator: Her voice dropped. The air between them felt heavier somehow.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Bold for emphasis, and a short narrator beat to sell the tension.
Lavender: Notice how the expression changed too — the show command before the line sets the mood visually.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_mystery
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Did you hear that just now? That {i}sound{/i}...
narrator: {color=#8877bb}Something stirred in the shadows at the edge of the room.{/color}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Italics for uncertainty, a muted color for the narrator's eerie description.
Lavender: And I moved to the right — staging matters as much as the words.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Cheerful scenes are all about warmth — bright colors, light narration, open expressions.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Drama lives in restraint. Short sentences, bold key words, and let the pauses do the heavy lifting.
[else]
Lavender: Mystery is about what you {i}don't{/i} say. Muted colors and indirect descriptions keep the reader guessing.
[endif]
Lavender: Open this file in the editor and try changing the lines yourself — that's the fastest way to learn.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
